        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 966,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Marks the elements that are nan, and counts them.\n\nnan (\"not a number\") comes from 0 / 0, from sqrt or log of a negative number, from a sensor that lost track. Infinity comes from dividing by zero or from exp of something large. Either spreads through everything computed from it - a mean, a filter's memory, a plot's range - so it is worth finding and replacing early (t.nan_to_num, t.where).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.isnan\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue or false for each element, the input's shape.\n\ncount:\nHow many are true. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nOf 1 nan 3 inf -inf 0, one is nan. Infinity is not nan: it is a number, only too large.\n\nRELATED:\nt.isnan, t.isfinite and t.isinf are the three tests. t.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity; t.where replaces them with anything. np.isnan and np.isfinite are the numpy versions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 950,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Marks the elements that are nan, and counts them.\n\nnan (\"not a number\") comes from 0 / 0, from sqrt or log of a negative number, from a sensor that lost track. Infinity comes from dividing by zero or from exp of something large. Either spreads through everything computed from it - a mean, a filter's memory, a plot's range - so it is worth finding and replacing early (t.nan_to_num, t.where).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.isnan\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmask:\nTrue or false for each element, the input's shape.\n\ncount:\nHow many are true. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nOf 1 nan 3 inf -inf 0, one is nan. Infinity is not nan: it is a number, only too large.\n\nRELATED:\nt.isnan, t.isfinite and t.isinf are the three tests. t.nan_to_num replaces nan and infinity; t.where replaces them with anything. np.isnan and np.isfinite are the numpy versions."
        },
